window.LESSON_CONTENT = window.LESSON_CONTENT || {};
window.LESSON_CONTENT['design'] = {"0":"<p>In this lesson, you will explore the three foundational principles that every professional graphic designer relies on: balance, contrast, and hierarchy. Understanding these principles matters whether you are designing a church announcement flyer in Monrovia, a business card for a market vendor in Kakata, or a social media post for an NGO working in rural Bong County. These principles are the invisible grammar of visual communication -- when applied correctly, your designs feel professional and trustworthy; when ignored, they feel confusing and amateurish.</p>\n\n<h4>Balance</h4>\n<p>Balance refers to how visual weight is distributed across a design. Elements carry visual weight based on their size, color, and density. There are two main types: <strong>symmetrical balance</strong>, where elements mirror each other on both sides (like a formal government letterhead), and <strong>asymmetrical balance</strong>, where different elements balance each other through contrast of size and color. A large photograph of a Liberian farmer on the left side of a poster can be balanced by a bold headline and a colored call-to-action button on the right -- even though the two sides look different, they carry equal visual weight and the design feels stable.</p>\n\n<h4>Contrast and Hierarchy</h4>\n<p>Contrast is the difference between elements -- dark vs. light, large vs. small, bold vs. thin. Contrast creates visual interest and draws the eye to important content. Hierarchy is the order in which a viewer's eye travels through a design. A skilled designer uses size, color, and placement to make the most important information the first thing seen. Think of a concert poster for an Afrobeats show in Paynesville: the artist's name is enormous, the event date is medium-sized, and the ticket price is small -- that deliberate sizing creates a clear reading order that guides every viewer naturally through the information.</p>\n\n<p>Consider a practical scenario from Liberia: a student at the University of Liberia is designing a recruitment flyer for a community clean-up event in Congo Town. If she places the event title, the date, the meeting location, and her phone number all in the same font size, viewers will not know where to look first. By applying hierarchy -- making the event title 48pt bold, the date 24pt, and the phone number 14pt -- she instantly creates a design that communicates clearly even to someone glancing at it for just two seconds on a crowded street.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Symmetrical Balance:</strong> Elements mirror each other across a central axis, creating a formal and stable feeling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Asymmetrical Balance:</strong> Different elements balance each other through contrasting size, color, or weight -- feels dynamic and modern.</li>\n  <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> The deliberate difference between elements (size, color, weight) that makes designs visually interesting and readable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Visual Hierarchy:</strong> The organized arrangement of elements so viewers read the most important information first and secondary details next.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, find any printed flyer, poster, or pamphlet around you -- from a church, market, or school -- and identify where the designer used (or failed to use) balance, contrast, and hierarchy. Write down two things the designer did well and one thing you would change to improve the design.</p>","1":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how color works as a communication tool -- not just decoration. Color theory is the science and art of understanding how colors relate to each other and how they affect human psychology. For designers in Liberia and West Africa, mastering color is especially powerful because color choices can reflect cultural identity, build brand trust, and make designs stand out in both digital and print environments. A business that chooses its colors thoughtlessly looks unprofessional; a business that chooses intentionally looks established and credible.</p>\n\n<h4>Color Psychology</h4>\n<p>Different colors trigger different emotional and psychological responses. <strong>Red</strong> communicates urgency, passion, and energy -- it is often used in sale announcements and emergency notices. <strong>Green</strong> suggests growth, health, and nature -- well suited for agricultural businesses, health clinics, or environmental organizations, which are all prominent across Liberia. <strong>Blue</strong> conveys trust, professionalism, and reliability -- that is why banks, tech companies, and government institutions worldwide lean heavily on blue. <strong>Yellow</strong> is optimistic and attention-grabbing but can feel overwhelming if overused. Understanding these associations helps you choose colors that reinforce your message rather than accidentally contradict it.</p>\n\n<h4>Color Palettes and the Color Wheel</h4>\n<p>A color palette is the set of colors you use consistently across a design or brand. Palettes are built using relationships on the color wheel. A <strong>complementary palette</strong> uses two colors directly opposite each other on the wheel (like orange and blue) for high contrast and energy. An <strong>analogous palette</strong> uses colors that sit next to each other (like green, teal, and blue) for a harmonious, calming effect. A <strong>monochromatic palette</strong> uses one base color in different shades and tints, creating elegant and cohesive designs. Tools like Canva's color palette generator and Adobe Color (color.adobe.com) allow you to build and save professional palettes in minutes.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a real-world example: a Liberian entrepreneur launching a fresh juice business in Red Light Market wants her brand to feel natural, healthy, and vibrant. She could choose an analogous palette of deep green, lime green, and golden yellow -- green for health and nature, yellow for energy and the tropical fruits she sells. If she also wants a bold contrast for her call-to-action buttons on social media, she could add a complementary orange. This kind of intentional color strategy is what separates professional designers from people who just pick colors they personally like.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Hue:</strong> The pure color itself (red, blue, green) without any white or black added.</li>\n  <li><strong>Saturation:</strong> The intensity or vividness of a color -- highly saturated colors are bright and bold; desaturated colors appear muted or grey.</li>\n  <li><strong>Complementary Colors:</strong> Colors directly opposite on the color wheel that create maximum contrast when paired.</li>\n  <li><strong>Brand Palette:</strong> The specific set of 2-4 colors chosen to represent a business or organization consistently across all materials.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, go to color.adobe.com or use Canva's palette tool and create a 4-color brand palette for an imaginary Liberian business of your choice (a restaurant, a school, a clinic, or a tech company). Write a one-sentence explanation for why you chose each color.</p>","2":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to use typography -- the art of arranging text -- to make your designs clear, professional, and visually appealing. Typography is one of the most powerful and most misused tools in graphic design. In Liberia and across West Africa, you can immediately spot an unprofessional design by its chaotic mix of fonts, tiny unreadable text, or decorative scripts used in places where clarity is essential. When you master typography, your designs immediately look more polished and your messages communicate more effectively to your audience.</p>\n\n<h4>Font Categories and When to Use Them</h4>\n<p>Fonts fall into four main categories. <strong>Serif fonts</strong> (like Times New Roman or Georgia) have small decorative strokes at the ends of letters -- they feel traditional, authoritative, and trustworthy, making them ideal for law firms, government documents, and formal invitations. <strong>Sans-serif fonts</strong> (like Arial, Helvetica, or Poppins) are clean and modern without the decorative strokes -- they are the most readable in digital environments and are widely used for tech companies, apps, and youth-focused brands. <strong>Display or decorative fonts</strong> are highly stylized and are appropriate only for headlines and logos, never for body text. <strong>Script fonts</strong> mimic handwriting and feel personal and elegant but must be used sparingly because they are harder to read at small sizes.</p>\n\n<h4>Font Pairing and Text Hierarchy</h4>\n<p>Great designers rarely use more than two or three fonts in a single design. The classic rule of font pairing is to combine a bold or distinctive font for headlines with a clean, readable font for body text. A popular professional pairing used across African NGO and tech brand materials is Playfair Display (a decorative serif for headlines) paired with Source Sans Pro (a clean sans-serif for body copy). Within a single font family, you create hierarchy using size, weight (bold vs. regular vs. light), and case (uppercase headlines vs. mixed-case body text). Consistency in your font choices across all materials is what builds a recognizable brand identity.</p>\n\n<p>Consider this scenario: a graphic design student in Buchanan, Grand Bassa County, is designing a school report card template for a local primary school. She uses a bold, professional sans-serif like Montserrat for the school name at the top, a clean regular-weight font like Open Sans for category headers and labels, and the same font in a smaller, lighter weight for the actual grade values. The result looks organized, official, and easy for parents to read -- exactly what a school document should feel like. The typography choices alone make the document look credible and well-designed.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Serif Font:</strong> A font with small decorative strokes on letter ends -- conveys tradition and authority (e.g., Georgia, Merriweather).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sans-serif Font:</strong> A clean font without decorative strokes -- modern and highly readable on screens (e.g., Poppins, Lato, Montserrat).</li>\n  <li><strong>Leading:</strong> The vertical spacing between lines of text -- proper leading (around 1.4-1.6x the font size) makes paragraphs comfortable to read.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kerning:</strong> The spacing between individual letter pairs -- poor kerning makes words look broken or awkward, especially in logos and headlines.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, open Canva and create a simple one-page event announcement for any event in your community. Use exactly two fonts -- one for the headline and one for all body text -- and apply size hierarchy so viewers read the event name first, the date second, and the location third.</p>","4":"<p>In this lesson, you will get hands-on with Canva -- one of the most widely used design tools in Africa and across the world. Canva is a free, browser-based design platform that allows anyone to create professional graphics without needing years of technical training. For students and small business owners in Liberia, Canva is especially valuable because it works on both computers and smartphones, requires no software installation, and offers thousands of templates for every type of design project you will encounter in real professional life. This lesson walks you through the Canva interface and shows you how to set up a Brand Kit so all your designs stay consistent.</p>\n\n<h4>Navigating the Canva Interface</h4>\n<p>When you open Canva, the homepage shows your recent designs and a search bar at the top. Clicking \"Create a design\" lets you choose a canvas size -- Canva offers presets for Instagram posts (1080x1080px), A4 documents, presentation slides, Facebook covers, and many more. Inside the editor, the left sidebar gives you access to Templates, Elements (shapes, icons, illustrations), Text boxes, Images (including a large free photo library), and Uploads (where you can bring in your own photos or logos). The top toolbar changes based on what you have selected -- click a text box and you see font options; click an image and you see crop and filter tools. Spending 20 minutes exploring these panels before you start designing will save you hours of confusion later.</p>\n\n<h4>Setting Up a Brand Kit</h4>\n<p>The Brand Kit feature (available in Canva Free with some limitations, and fully unlocked in Canva Pro) is where you store your brand's colors, fonts, and logo so they are always one click away in any design. To set it up, go to the Brand Kit section in your Canva dashboard, upload your logo, add your brand's hex color codes (for example, #1A3C6E for a deep navy blue), and select your brand fonts. From that point forward, whenever you open a new design, your brand colors and fonts appear at the top of the color and font menus. For a Liberian startup or community organization managing multiple social media accounts, this feature alone saves enormous time and ensures every post, flyer, and banner looks unified and professional.</p>\n\n<p>Imagine a youth-led organization in Nimba County running a digital literacy program. They use Canva to create weekly social media posts, event flyers, and donor reports. Without a Brand Kit, each designer on their volunteer team might use slightly different shades of their green brand color and different fonts, making the brand look inconsistent and unprofessional to donors and partners. With a Brand Kit configured, every team member automatically applies the correct colors and fonts -- the brand looks the same whether the post was made by the director or a student volunteer.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Canvas Size:</strong> The dimensions of your design file -- always match the platform's recommended size (e.g., 1080x1080px for Instagram square posts).</li>\n  <li><strong>Brand Kit:</strong> A saved collection of your organization's official colors, fonts, and logos accessible in every Canva design.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hex Code:</strong> A 6-digit code (like #FF5733) that precisely identifies a color -- used to ensure your exact brand color is used every time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Template:</strong> A pre-designed layout you can customize with your own content -- a major time-saver for creating professional designs quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a free Canva account if you do not already have one, then set up a Brand Kit for a real or imaginary Liberian business or organization. Add at least two brand colors (using specific hex codes), select two fonts, and upload a simple placeholder logo. Take a screenshot of your completed Brand Kit page.</p>","5":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to design logos, flyers, and print materials using Canva -- the three most in-demand design deliverables for small businesses and organizations across Liberia and West Africa. Whether a church in Sinkor needs a Sunday program, a restaurant in Waterside needs a menu, or a startup in the Freeport area needs a business card, knowing how to produce clean, print-ready materials in Canva is a skill that can immediately generate income and community impact. This lesson covers the essential settings, techniques, and design decisions that make print materials look truly professional.</p>\n\n<h4>Designing Logos in Canva</h4>\n<p>A logo is the visual identity of an organization -- it must be simple, memorable, and versatile enough to work on a small business card and a large banner. In Canva, start your logo by selecting the \"Logo\" canvas size (500x500px by default). The best logos use a combination of an icon or symbol and a wordmark (the business name in a distinctive font). Keep your logo to two colors maximum so it remains recognizable and printable in black-and-white. Canva's Elements library has thousands of icons you can use as starting points -- search for icons relevant to your industry, like a leaf for a natural foods business or a book for an educational institution. Always download your final logo as a PNG with a transparent background so it can be placed on any colored surface without a white box around it.</p>\n\n<h4>Flyers and Print Materials</h4>\n<p>When designing for print in Canva, choose the correct paper size from the start -- A4 (210x297mm) is standard in Liberia for most flyers, certificates, and documents. For business cards, the standard size is 3.5 x 2 inches (88.9 x 50.8mm). Always use Canva's \"Print Bleed\" option when your design has color or images that extend to the edge of the page -- bleed adds a small extra margin (usually 3mm) so that when the printer cuts the paper, no white edges appear. Place your most critical information (business name, phone number, key message) well within the safe zone -- at least 5mm from the edge -- to ensure nothing important gets accidentally trimmed.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a practical example: a Liberian entrepreneur named James runs a printing and stationery shop in Monrovia's Broad Street business district. His clients often come to him with Canva-designed files that are the wrong size, have no bleed, or use low-resolution images that look blurry when printed. Understanding the difference between a screen-ready and print-ready design file would save his clients money and ensure they get quality results. When you design with proper bleed, correct dimensions, and high-resolution images (300 DPI), your file is ready to hand to any printer in Liberia or to order online from international print services.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Bleed:</strong> Extra image or color extending 3mm beyond the trim edge of a print document, preventing white borders after cutting.</li>\n  <li><strong>Safe Zone:</strong> The inner area of a print design (5mm from the edge) where all critical text and logos must stay to avoid being trimmed off.</li>\n  <li><strong>Transparent Background (PNG):</strong> A logo file format where the background is invisible, allowing the logo to be placed on any color without a white box.</li>\n  <li><strong>Wordmark:</strong> A logo style where the business name itself, set in a distinctive font, forms the primary visual identity.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, design a one-sided A4 flyer in Canva for a real or imaginary event or business in your community. Enable the print bleed setting, keep all key text within the safe zone, and download the final file as a PDF (Print) to experience the print-ready export process.</p>","6":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to design social media graphics and animated posts using Canva -- one of the most practically useful skills for anyone running a business, organization, or personal brand in today's digital Liberia. Social media platforms like Facebook, WhatsApp, Instagram, and TikTok are now the primary way businesses and communities across Monrovia and the rest of Liberia communicate, advertise, and build audience. A business that posts professionally designed graphics consistently looks more credible and attracts more customers than one posting blurry photos with messy text overlays. This lesson will show you exactly how to create graphics that stand out in crowded social media feeds.</p>\n\n<h4>Platform Sizes and Design Rules for Social Media</h4>\n<p>Every social media platform has specific recommended dimensions for different types of posts. <strong>Instagram and WhatsApp square posts</strong> use 1080x1080px. <strong>Instagram Stories and WhatsApp Status</strong> use 1080x1920px (a vertical 9:16 ratio). <strong>Facebook event covers</strong> use 1920x1005px. <strong>Twitter/X post images</strong> use 1600x900px. Using the wrong dimensions causes your images to be cropped, stretched, or displayed with ugly borders -- immediately making your design look unprofessional. In Canva, simply search for the platform name when creating a new design and it will automatically set the correct canvas size. Designing for mobile-first is especially important in Liberia, where the majority of internet users access social media on smartphones, not computers.</p>\n\n<h4>Animated Posts in Canva</h4>\n<p>Canva allows you to add animation to any design element -- text, images, shapes, and backgrounds -- turning a static graphic into an eye-catching animated post. Click on any element, then select \"Animate\" from the top toolbar. You can choose from animations like \"Rise\" (elements slide up into place), \"Fade\" (elements gently appear), or \"Pop\" (elements bounce in with energy). For the entire page, you can apply \"Page transitions\" that animate how the design appears when viewed. Once your animated design is ready, export it as an MP4 video or a GIF file. Animated posts receive significantly higher engagement on Facebook and Instagram than static images -- this is well documented by social media marketing research across Africa and globally.</p>\n\n<p>Consider this scenario: a fashion boutique in Monrovia called Kpelle Style wants to announce a new clothing collection on Instagram. A static post with a photo of the clothes and some text is decent, but an animated post where the business name fades in first, then the clothing photos slide in from the sides, then a \"Shop Now\" button pops up at the bottom, creates a dynamic, professional advertisement that stops people from scrolling past. Using Canva's animation tools, this effect takes about 10 additional minutes beyond creating the static version -- and the result looks like something produced by a professional advertising agency.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aspect Ratio:</strong> The proportional relationship between a design's width and height -- social media platforms have strict aspect ratio requirements (e.g., 1:1 for square, 9:16 for stories).</li>\n  <li><strong>Animation:</strong> Movement applied to design elements in Canva that plays when the post is viewed as a video or GIF.</li>\n  <li><strong>GIF:</strong> A short looping animated image format -- good for subtle animations; smaller file size than video.</li>\n  <li><strong>Engagement Rate:</strong> The percentage of people who see a social media post and interact with it (likes, comments, shares) -- animated content typically drives higher engagement.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a square Instagram post (1080x1080px) in Canva for a product, event, or announcement relevant to your life or community. Add at least two animated elements using the Animate tool, then export it as an MP4. Post it or share it with a classmate and note how it looks on a smartphone screen.</p>","8":"<p>In this lesson, you will take your first steps inside Adobe Photoshop -- the industry-standard software for professional photo editing, digital art, and advanced graphic design. While Canva is excellent for quick, template-based design work, Photoshop gives you complete creative control over every pixel in an image. For designers in Liberia and across Africa who want to work with international clients, advertising agencies, NGOs, or media organizations, Photoshop proficiency is a highly valued skill that significantly increases your earning potential. This lesson introduces the core Photoshop interface and the essential tools you will use in almost every project.</p>\n\n<h4>The Photoshop Workspace</h4>\n<p>When you first open Photoshop, you see the main canvas in the center, the <strong>Tools panel</strong> on the left (a vertical strip of icons for every editing action), the <strong>Options bar</strong> at the top (which changes based on your selected tool), and the <strong>Panels area</strong> on the right, which includes the critically important <strong>Layers panel</strong>. The Layers panel is where every element of your design lives as a separate, editable stack -- think of layers like transparent sheets of glass piled on top of each other. You can move, hide, or edit each layer independently without affecting anything else in the design. Understanding layers is the single most important concept for working efficiently in Photoshop.</p>\n\n<h4>Essential Tools Every Designer Must Know</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Move Tool (V)</strong> lets you drag any layer to reposition it on the canvas. The <strong>Marquee Tool (M)</strong> makes rectangular or elliptical selections. The <strong>Lasso Tool (L)</strong> lets you draw a free-form selection around irregular shapes. The <strong>Magic Wand Tool (W)</strong> selects areas of similar color with a single click -- useful for quickly removing solid-color backgrounds. The <strong>Crop Tool (C)</strong> trims your canvas to a new size or ratio. The <strong>Brush Tool (B)</strong> paints on the canvas or on layer masks. The <strong>Type Tool (T)</strong> adds text. The <strong>Zoom Tool (Z)</strong> magnifies your view. Learning the single-key keyboard shortcuts (V, M, L, W, C, B, T, Z) dramatically speeds up your workflow because you can switch tools without taking your hands off the keyboard.</p>\n\n<p>Here is how Photoshop skills translate to real opportunity in Liberia: a photographer covering events at the University of Liberia shoots hundreds of photos at graduation ceremonies. Many photos have distracting backgrounds, poor lighting, or need the university banner straightened and the colors enhanced before being published in the school newsletter or posted online. With Photoshop, this photographer can process an entire batch of graduation photos in an afternoon, delivering polished, professional images that reflect well on both the university and his photography business. These same skills apply to product photography for local businesses, portrait editing, and documentary photography for NGOs.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Layer:</strong> An independent editable element in a Photoshop file -- keeping elements on separate layers allows non-destructive editing and easy adjustments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Selection:</strong> A defined area of the canvas that limits editing actions to only that region -- fundamental to precise photo editing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Non-destructive Editing:</strong> Making changes that can be undone or adjusted later without permanently altering the original image data (using adjustment layers, smart objects, and layer masks).</li>\n  <li><strong>PSD File:</strong> Photoshop's native file format (.psd) that preserves all layers, masks, and settings -- always save your working file as a PSD before exporting the final image.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, open any photo in Photoshop and practice using at least five different tools from the Tools panel. Use the Crop tool to recompose the photo, add a text layer with the Type tool, and move elements using the Move tool. Save the file as a PSD to preserve your layers.</p>","9":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to retouch photos, correct colors, and apply filters in Adobe Photoshop -- skills that transform an ordinary photograph into a compelling, professional image. Photo retouching and color correction are among the most commercially requested Photoshop skills in Liberia and across Africa, from portrait photographers and wedding videographers to product photographers and social media managers working for businesses that need polished visuals. Understanding these techniques allows you to fix real-world problems: photos taken in poor lighting, skin tones that look washed out on camera, product images with dull colors, or portraits with blemishes that need to be professionally removed.</p>\n\n<h4>Color Correction with Adjustment Layers</h4>\n<p>Color correction is the process of fixing the color balance of a photo to make it look natural and accurate. The best way to do this in Photoshop is with <strong>Adjustment Layers</strong> (found under Layer > New Adjustment Layer or via the Adjustments panel). The most important adjustment tools are: <strong>Levels</strong>, which allows you to set the darkest blacks, brightest whites, and midtone balance in your image; <strong>Curves</strong>, which gives you more precise control over tonal ranges and is used to add contrast and richness; and <strong>Hue/Saturation</strong>, which lets you shift, boost, or reduce any color in the image. Adjustment layers are non-destructive -- they sit above your photo layer and can be turned off or modified at any time without permanently changing the original photo.</p>\n\n<h4>Retouching Tools: Spot Healing, Clone Stamp, and Dodge/Burn</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Spot Healing Brush (J)</strong> automatically removes blemishes, dust spots, and small imperfections by analyzing the surrounding texture and filling in the selected area seamlessly. The <strong>Clone Stamp Tool (S)</strong> copies pixels from one part of the image to another -- useful for removing larger distracting objects or repairing damaged areas of old photographs. <strong>Dodge</strong> and <strong>Burn</strong> tools (O) lighten and darken specific areas of a photo respectively -- for example, you can use Dodge to brighten the whites of a subject's eyes, or Burn to add subtle shadow definition to facial features for a more sculpted portrait look.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a real scenario: a Liberian business owner in Paynesville is selling handmade leather bags online and on WhatsApp. She photographs her products with her smartphone but the images look flat, slightly yellow from indoor lighting, and the background is a cluttered room. In Photoshop, she can use a Curves adjustment layer to add contrast and richness, shift the white balance using Hue/Saturation to remove the yellow cast, and use the Magic Wand and Layer Mask to replace the background with a clean white studio-style background. The result is a product photo that looks professional enough to compete with items listed on international e-commerce platforms, dramatically increasing her sales credibility.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Adjustment Layer:</strong> A non-destructive editing layer that applies color and tonal corrections above the image without permanently altering pixel data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Spot Healing Brush:</strong> A tool that automatically patches small blemishes or imperfections by blending surrounding pixels intelligently.</li>\n  <li><strong>White Balance:</strong> The color temperature correction that makes whites appear truly white regardless of the light source color when the photo was taken.</li>\n  <li><strong>Layer Mask:</strong> A grayscale map attached to a layer that controls which areas are visible (white) or hidden (black) -- the primary tool for non-destructive background removal.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, take a photo of any person or product -- your own or a free stock photo -- and use Photoshop to complete three corrections: apply a Curves adjustment layer to improve contrast, use the Spot Healing Brush to remove at least one imperfection, and adjust the Hue/Saturation to make the colors appear more vivid and natural.</p>","10":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to create composites -- designs that combine multiple images into one believable, seamless scene -- and how to use Photoshop's advanced selection tools to make precise, professional cutouts. These techniques are used every day in advertising, editorial design, and digital art across the world, and the demand for these skills is growing rapidly in West Africa as brands invest more in digital advertising. Compositing is what allows advertisers to place a product into a dramatic environment, create fantasy scenes, or build promotional images that would be impossible or too expensive to photograph in real life.</p>\n\n<h4>Advanced Selection Tools</h4>\n<p>Making a clean, precise selection is the foundation of all compositing work. Beyond the basic Marquee and Lasso tools, Photoshop offers powerful advanced tools: the <strong>Quick Selection Tool (W)</strong> paints a selection that automatically follows the edges of objects as you drag across them. The <strong>Select Subject</strong> button (found in the top toolbar when a selection tool is active) uses Adobe's AI to automatically detect and select the main subject of a photo with impressive accuracy -- extremely useful for cutting out people and objects. The <strong>Select and Mask workspace</strong> (accessible after making any selection via the top toolbar) provides the most advanced edge refinement tools, including the <strong>Refine Edge Brush</strong>, which can detect and preserve fine details like hair, fur, or feathers that would be impossible to select manually.</p>\n\n<h4>Building a Composite Image</h4>\n<p>Creating a composite involves several steps: first, make a clean selection of your subject and use a <strong>Layer Mask</strong> to hide the original background (masking is always preferred over deleting, because it is non-destructive). Second, place your subject into the new background image on a separate layer. Third, match the lighting and color of the subject to the new background -- this is where your color correction skills from the previous lesson become essential. Add a shadow beneath your subject (using a soft brush on a new layer set to Multiply blending mode) to ground it in the scene. Finally, apply a subtle overall color grade using a Curves or Color Balance adjustment layer clipped to affect all layers, so the entire composite feels like one unified photograph.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a practical example relevant to Liberia: a graphic designer working for a telecommunications company in Monrovia needs to create a billboard advertisement showing a young Liberian student using a new mobile data plan while standing in front of an inspiring cityscape of Monrovia at sunset. The photographer shoots the student against a plain white backdrop in a studio. The designer then uses Select Subject to cut out the student precisely, places her against a panoramic Monrovia skyline image, matches the warm sunset lighting by adjusting her layer's Color Balance, adds a ground shadow, and composites the company's logo and promotional text. The final billboard image looks completely natural and professionally produced -- that is the power of compositing.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Composite:</strong> A single design image created by combining elements from multiple photographs or graphics into one believable unified scene.</li>\n  <li><strong>Select Subject:</strong> An AI-powered Photoshop feature that automatically detects and selects the main subject in a photo with a single click.</li>\n  <li><strong>Refine Edge Brush:</strong> A tool in the Select and Mask workspace that intelligently detects and preserves fine edge details like hair when making cutout selections.</li>\n  <li><strong>Blending Mode:</strong> A layer setting that controls how that layer's pixels interact with the layers below -- Multiply darkens (good for shadows), Screen lightens, Overlay adds contrast.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, find two free stock photos -- one of a person or object and one of a landscape or interior background. Use Select Subject and the Select and Mask workspace to cut out the person or object, place it onto the new background, add a shadow layer in Multiply mode, and apply one Curves adjustment layer to unify the color of the entire composite.</p>","12":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn the technical requirements for designing materials that will be physically printed -- understanding resolution, bleed, and the CMYK color model. These technical concepts are the difference between a design that looks beautiful on your screen and one that actually prints correctly. Many designers in Liberia and across West Africa have experienced the frustration of sending a design to a printer only to receive blurry output, unexpected white borders, or colors that look completely different from the screen. This lesson explains exactly why those problems happen and how to prevent them every time.</p>\n\n<h4>Resolution: DPI and Why It Matters</h4>\n<p><strong>Resolution</strong> refers to the density of pixels in an image, measured in <strong>DPI (dots per inch)</strong>. Screen displays use 72-96 DPI, which is fine for websites and social media. Print materials require a minimum of <strong>300 DPI</strong> at the final print size -- this means your image contains 300 pixels for every inch of printed space, resulting in sharp, crisp detail. If you use a 72 DPI screen image in a print design and enlarge it to fill an A4 page, the printer will reveal every pixel as a blocky, blurry square. When you start a print project in Photoshop, always set the resolution to 300 DPI in the New Document dialog. When sourcing images, always verify the image dimensions -- a photo that is 1200x900px at 300 DPI gives you only a 4x3 inch print area; for a full A4 page at 300 DPI, you need an image that is at least 2480x3508px.</p>\n\n<h4>Bleed and CMYK Color Mode</h4>\n<p><strong>Bleed</strong> is the extra artwork that extends beyond the trim edge of your printed piece -- standard bleed is 3mm on all sides. When commercial printers cut printed sheets to size, there is a small margin of error in the cutting machine. Bleed ensures that if the cut lands slightly outside the trim line, there is no white paper showing at the edge. Always extend any background color, pattern, or image that reaches the edge of your design into the bleed area. <strong>CMYK</strong> (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Key/Black) is the color model used by commercial printers -- physical ink is mixed from these four colors to create the full spectrum of print colors. Computer screens use <strong>RGB</strong> (Red, Green, Blue) light to display colors, and RGB colors appear more vibrant than their CMYK equivalents. Always convert your Photoshop or print design file to CMYK mode (Image > Mode > CMYK Color) before sending to a printer to avoid unexpected color shifts.</p>\n\n<p>Consider this real-world scenario in Liberia: a community health organization in Margibi County commissions a designer to create 500 copies of a health awareness brochure. The designer produces a beautiful layout in RGB mode with vivid red accents and sends the PDF to a local print shop in Kakata. When the brochures arrive, the vivid red has printed as a dull orange-brown, and one side of every brochure has a thin white stripe along the edge because the background color did not extend into the bleed. Both problems are entirely preventable -- CMYK conversion for the color shift, and proper 3mm bleed for the white edge. The organization has wasted money on a reprint, which a professional designer would have avoided from the start.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>DPI (Dots Per Inch):</strong> The resolution measurement for print -- all print designs require a minimum of 300 DPI to appear sharp and clear when physically printed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bleed:</strong> The 3mm extension of artwork beyond the trim line that prevents white edges from appearing when commercial printers cut the paper.</li>\n  <li><strong>CMYK:</strong> The four-ink color model (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Black) used by commercial printers -- always work in CMYK mode for any design going to print.</li>\n  <li><strong>Trim Line:</strong> The line marking the actual final size of the printed piece after cutting -- your bleed extends beyond it, your safe zone stays inside it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, open Photoshop and create a new document for an A4 print flyer: set the size to 210x297mm, resolution to 300 DPI, color mode to CMYK, and add 3mm bleed. Design a simple one-color background that extends into the bleed area, add a text headline, and export it as a PDF (Print) to see the bleed guides included in the exported file.</p>","13":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn the specific technical requirements and design best practices for creating graphics that will live on screens -- websites, email newsletters, and social media platforms. Digital design has different rules from print design: you work in RGB color, at 72-96 DPI, and you must constantly consider how your design will look on different screen sizes, from desktop monitors to the small smartphone screens used by the majority of internet users across Liberia and West Africa. Mastering digital design specifications ensures your work looks intentional and polished, not stretched, blurry, or wrongly cropped.</p>\n\n<h4>Web and Email Design Specifications</h4>\n<p>For websites, graphics are always created in <strong>RGB color mode</strong> at <strong>72 DPI</strong> -- higher resolution is unnecessary because screens cannot display more than their native pixel density, and larger file sizes only slow down page loading. For web, file formats matter: use <strong>JPEG</strong> for photographs (good compression, small file size, slight quality loss), <strong>PNG</strong> for graphics with transparency or sharp edges like logos and icons (lossless quality), and <strong>SVG</strong> for icons and simple illustrations that need to scale perfectly at any size. For email newsletters -- widely used by NGOs, schools, and businesses in Liberia -- the standard email width is 600px, and a hero banner image at the top is typically 600x200px to 600x300px. Keep total email image file sizes small (under 1MB total) because many users in West Africa access email on mobile data connections where large files load slowly.</p>\n\n<h4>Social Media Image Specifications</h4>\n<p>Each social media platform has specific recommended image sizes that change periodically, but the core sizes to know are: Instagram feed posts at <strong>1080x1080px</strong> (square) or 1080x1350px (portrait), Instagram and WhatsApp Stories at <strong>1080x1920px</strong>, Facebook post images at <strong>1200x630px</strong>, and LinkedIn company post images at <strong>1200x627px</strong>. When designing for social media, always design with the assumption that text may be overlaid with platform UI elements -- Instagram adds the username and interaction buttons over the bottom portion of images, and LinkedIn places a description below. Keep critical content in the center and upper portion of your designs. Also design for <strong>thumb-stopping impact</strong> -- your graphic has less than 2 seconds to capture attention as someone scrolls on their phone, so bold colors, clear headlines, and strong visuals are more important in social media graphics than in any other format.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a relevant example: a Liberian human rights organization based in Monrovia runs monthly email updates to their international donors and a weekly Facebook and WhatsApp campaign for local community awareness. Their digital designer uses a 600px-wide email template with a bold header image, minimal text-in-image (because some email clients block images), and clear HTML text and call-to-action buttons that work even when images are disabled. For their Facebook and WhatsApp posts, the same designer creates 1080x1080px graphics with the organization's brand colors, a striking headline in large text, and a simple strong image -- knowing the post will be viewed primarily on phone screens in varying light conditions across rural and urban Liberia.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>RGB:</strong> The Red-Green-Blue color model used for all digital screen displays -- always use RGB for web, email, and social media designs.</li>\n  <li><strong>JPEG vs PNG:</strong> JPEG compresses photos efficiently for fast loading; PNG preserves transparency and sharp edges for logos, icons, and graphics -- choose based on image type, not preference.</li>\n  <li><strong>Responsive Design:</strong> Designing with the awareness that your graphic will be viewed on many different screen sizes -- prioritize mobile viewing since most West African internet users are on smartphones.</li>\n  <li><strong>File Size Optimization:</strong> Compressing digital images to the smallest file size that still looks visually clean -- critical for users on limited mobile data connections.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a set of three versions of the same design -- one sized for an Instagram square post (1080x1080px), one for a WhatsApp Status (1080x1920px), and one for a Facebook post (1200x630px) -- all for the same fictional event or business. This exercise builds the habit of designing for multiple platforms from a single concept.</p>","14":"<p>In this lesson, you will learn how to build a professional design portfolio and present your work effectively to clients, employers, and the creative community. Your portfolio is the single most important tool for your design career -- it is more important than your resume, your degree, or your years of experience. For young designers in Liberia and across West Africa, a strong online portfolio creates opportunities that geography and limited local industry connections cannot: international clients, remote freelance work, internship applications at NGOs and media companies, and collaborations with designers across the continent. This lesson shows you exactly what to include, how to organize it, and how to present it professionally.</p>\n\n<h4>What to Include in Your Portfolio</h4>\n<p>A strong design portfolio contains <strong>8 to 15 of your best projects</strong> -- not everything you have ever made. Quality always beats quantity. Each project should demonstrate a different skill or design type: include a logo design, a social media campaign, a print flyer or brochure, a photo-edited image, and if possible a multi-piece brand identity project. For each project, present not just the final design but also a brief explanation of the context: what was the client's problem, what design decisions did you make, and how does the final design solve that problem. This \"case study\" format is the standard used by professional designers worldwide and immediately distinguishes your portfolio from one that simply shows pictures of finished work with no explanation.</p>\n\n<h4>Portfolio Platforms and Presentation</h4>\n<p>The most widely used platforms for hosting design portfolios are <strong>Behance</strong> (owned by Adobe, free, and used by millions of designers globally -- showing your work here increases discoverability significantly), <strong>Canva's portfolio feature</strong> (quick to set up and well-integrated if you already use Canva), and personal websites built with tools like <strong>Wix</strong> or <strong>Squarespace</strong> (the most professional option for established designers). For presenting your work in client meetings or interviews, create a polished PDF portfolio deck -- a 10-15 slide document that shows your best work with brief project descriptions, built in Canva or Photoshop. Keep your portfolio updated: add every significant new project within a week of completing it, and remove your weakest older work as your skills improve.</p>\n\n<p>Here is a real example of portfolio impact in a Liberian context: a graphic design graduate from the African Methodist Episcopal University in Monrovia builds a Behance portfolio with eight strong projects -- a logo for a fictional Liberian tech company, social media graphics for a community health campaign, a photo composite for an imaginary tourism advertisement featuring Sapo National Park, and a brand identity kit for a hypothetical restaurant on Mamba Point. Because Behance is indexed by Google and actively browsed by international creative directors, she begins receiving freelance inquiries from design agencies in Nigeria, Ghana, and the United Kingdom within months of publishing her portfolio -- work she accesses remotely from Monrovia. The portfolio made her talent globally visible in a way that local networking alone never could.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Portfolio:</strong> A curated collection of your best design work used to demonstrate skills and attract clients, employers, or collaborators.</li>\n  <li><strong>Case Study:</strong> A project presentation that explains the design problem, your creative decisions, and the final outcome -- far more compelling than showing the finished design alone.</li>\n  <li><strong>Behance:</strong> Adobe's free platform for publishing and discovering design portfolios -- one of the highest-traffic creative platforms in the world and important for discoverability.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mockup:</strong> A realistic template that displays your design applied to a real-world object (a phone screen, a t-shirt, a billboard) -- makes portfolio presentations far more compelling and professional.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, select three of your best designs created during this course and write a 3-sentence case study for each one explaining what the design was for, one key creative decision you made, and what you would improve if you did it again. Then upload all three to a free Behance account and publish your first professional portfolio project.</p>","15":"<p>This quiz covers <strong>Module 4: Professional Design & Portfolio</strong>, preparing artwork for print with CMYK, 300 DPI, bleed and safe areas; designing correctly for web, social media, and email; and building a portfolio that wins clients and jobs.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong>, with <strong>unlimited retries</strong>, so relax and think through each question. If print specs still feel confusing, rewatch lesson 4.1 before retrying. Master these production details and printers, clients, and employers will treat you as a true professional. You are nearly at the finish line!</p>","16":"<p>A logo is often a business's single most valuable design asset. Walk down Broad Street in Monrovia and you will see it instantly: businesses with a strong, consistent mark look established and trustworthy, while those with a blurry, cluttered logo struggle to be taken seriously. Professional designers do not \"just draw a logo\", they follow a repeatable process, and that process is what clients actually pay for.</p><h4>Step 1: Research Before You Design</h4><p>Start with a discovery conversation: What does the business do? Who are its customers? What feeling should the brand give, trustworthy like a bank, energetic like a phone shop, warm like a cook shop? Then study competitors. If every microfinance brand in Liberia uses blue circles, a distinctive shape or color helps your client stand out while staying appropriate.</p><h4>Step 2: Sketch Many, Choose Few</h4><p>Great logos begin on paper. Sketch 20-30 rough ideas quickly, words, symbols, initials, combinations. Quantity first, quality later. Then shortlist the 3 strongest concepts to develop digitally. Presenting exactly three refined concepts (not one, not fifteen) is an industry norm that gives clients real choice without overwhelming them.</p><h4>Step 3: Know the Logo Types</h4><ul><li><strong>Wordmark:</strong> the styled business name itself (like Coca-Cola).</li><li><strong>Lettermark:</strong> initials, useful for long names (like LEC).</li><li><strong>Symbol/icon:</strong> a graphic mark that works even without text.</li><li><strong>Combination mark:</strong> symbol plus name, the most versatile choice for most Liberian small businesses.</li></ul><h4>Step 4: Test for Real-World Strength</h4><p>A professional logo must pass practical tests: Does it stay clear in one color (for stamps, receipts, and cheap printing)? Is it legible at the size of a phone icon or a WhatsApp profile photo? Does it work on both light and dark backgrounds? Does it avoid fine details that vanish when a sign painter reproduces it on a shopfront?</p><h4>Vector Thinking</h4><p>Logos should ultimately exist as vector graphics, shapes defined by math, not pixels, so they scale from a business card to a billboard without blurring. Canva can export SVG, and Photoshop work should start large at high resolution.</p><p>Your practice task: run this full process for a fictional Liberian brand, research, 20 sketches, 3 digital concepts, and the one-color test. This disciplined process is exactly what you will present to paying clients in Module 6.</p>","17":"<p>A logo alone is not a brand. A <strong>brand identity system</strong> is the complete visual toolkit, colors, typography, imagery, and rules, that makes every flyer, receipt, sign, and Facebook post recognizably belong to one business. Consistency is why customers trust Orange or Ecobank materials at a glance, and it is a service you can sell to Liberian businesses at far higher value than a single logo.</p><h4>The Core Components</h4><ul><li><strong>Logo suite:</strong> the primary logo plus variations, a horizontal version, a stacked version, an icon-only mark for profile photos, and a one-color version for low-cost printing.</li><li><strong>Color palette:</strong> typically one primary color, one or two secondary colors, and neutrals. Record exact values: HEX for digital (like #E31E24), CMYK for print. \"Roughly red\" is not a brand color, #E31E24 is.</li><li><strong>Typography system:</strong> one font for headlines, one for body text, with rules for sizes and weights. Two fonts are usually enough; five is chaos.</li><li><strong>Imagery style:</strong> guidance on photos and graphics, for example, \"real photos of Liberian customers, bright natural light, no generic foreign stock images.\"</li></ul><h4>The Brand Guidelines Document</h4><p>Professionals deliver these decisions in a short PDF called <strong>brand guidelines</strong> (or a style guide): logo usage with minimum sizes and clear space, incorrect usage examples (never stretch, recolor, or add shadows), the palette with codes, the fonts, and one or two sample applications. For a small business, 6-10 pages is plenty. Canva's Brand Kit then stores these assets so every future design automatically pulls the right colors and fonts.</p><h4>Applying the System</h4><p>Show the identity working across real touchpoints: a business card, a flyer, a Facebook cover, a WhatsApp profile icon, a signboard concept, and a T-shirt. This is where clients fall in love, they stop seeing a logo and start seeing their business looking professional everywhere their customers meet it.</p><h4>Why This Matters for Your Career</h4><p>A designer who delivers \"a logo file\" earns a small one-time fee. A designer who delivers a logo suite, palette, fonts, guidelines PDF, and templates becomes the business's ongoing brand partner. In Module 6 you will price and package exactly this offer. For now, build a full identity system for the brand you started in lesson 5.1, it will become the centerpiece of your portfolio.</p>","18":"<p>Two designers can create equally good logos, yet one wins the client and the other does not. The difference is almost always <strong>presentation</strong>. Clients, a Paynesville restaurant owner, an NGO director, a church committee, cannot always imagine a flat logo on a white canvas becoming their real-world brand. Your job is to show them, and the tool for that is the mockup.</p><h4>What Mockups Do</h4><p>A mockup places your design onto a realistic photo: a signboard, a T-shirt, a business card held in a hand, a phone showing an Instagram profile. Mockups let the client emotionally experience their future brand. In Canva, search \"mockup\" in Elements or use Smart Mockups; in Photoshop, use mockup PSD files with smart objects, double-click the smart object layer, paste your design, save, and the design wraps realistically onto the product with correct lighting and perspective.</p><h4>Choose Context That Feels Local</h4><p>Show the brand where the client's customers will actually meet it: a storefront sign, a keke or taxi door, a market banner, a flyer on a notice board, a WhatsApp Business profile. A Monrovia cook shop owner will react far more strongly to their logo on a signboard photo than on an abstract gradient background.</p><h4>Structuring the Presentation</h4><p>Present brand work as a short, ordered story rather than a single image dump:</p><ul><li><strong>1. Recap the brief</strong>, one slide restating the client's goals in their own words.</li><li><strong>2. Show the concept</strong>, the logo large, on clean background, with a sentence on the thinking behind it.</li><li><strong>3. Show variations</strong>, color, one-color, and icon versions.</li><li><strong>4. Show mockups</strong>, 4-6 real-world applications.</li><li><strong>5. Close with next steps</strong>, feedback deadline and what final files they will receive.</li></ul><h4>Handling Feedback Professionally</h4><p>Ask guided questions (\"Does this feel trustworthy to your customers?\") instead of \"Do you like it?\". Separate useful direction from vague reactions, agree changes in writing, and stick to your included revision rounds. Never send editable working files until final payment, deliver PNGs and PDFs for review, source files on completion.</p><p>Assemble a full presentation for your Module 5 brand project. That deck, with mockups, is simultaneously your practice, your portfolio piece, and the template you will reuse for every paying client for years.</p>","19":"<p>This quiz covers <strong>Module 5: Brand Identity & Logo Design</strong>, the research-sketch-refine logo process, logo types and one-color testing, complete identity systems with exact color codes and font pairs, brand guidelines, and presenting work with mockups.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong>, and <strong>unlimited retries</strong> mean there is no pressure, every attempt teaches you something. If you hesitate on any answer, revisit the lesson and your own brand project. Designers who master this module stop selling logos and start selling brands. Well done for coming this far!</p>","20":"<p>Design skill becomes income the moment you can find a client, agree a fair price, and protect yourself with clear terms. In Liberia the demand is real: businesses need flyers and logos, churches need event graphics, NGOs need report layouts and campaign visuals, and political seasons create waves of design work. This lesson turns you from \"someone who designs\" into someone businesses hire with confidence.</p><h4>Finding Your First Clients</h4><p>Start where trust already exists: your network, your church or mosque, market associations, and local Facebook and WhatsApp business groups. Offer a visible \"before and after\", redesign a real business's flyer free or cheap, post both versions with the owner's permission, and let the improvement advertise you. Two or three visible wins typically generate the first paid referrals.</p><h4>The Design Brief</h4><p>Never start work from a vague WhatsApp message. Capture in writing: the business and audience, the exact deliverables (\"one A5 flyer, front only, print-ready PDF plus a social media version\"), the content they will supply (text, photos, logo), the deadline, and the number of revision rounds included (two is standard). A one-page brief prevents the most common freelance disaster, endless free changes.</p><h4>Pricing Without Fear</h4><p>Beginners in the region commonly charge in ranges like $10-25 for a flyer, $50-150 for a logo, and $150-400 for a full brand identity package, rising with experience and portfolio strength. Three rules: price the value and usage, not just your hours; never work large jobs without a <strong>50% deposit</strong>; and put extra revisions beyond the agreed rounds at a stated fee. NGOs and companies expect invoices, a simple Canva-designed invoice with your name, service, amount, and payment details (bank or mobile money) makes you look established.</p><h4>Simple Written Agreements</h4><p>For anything beyond a small flyer, send a short agreement covering deliverables, price, deposit, timeline, revision rounds, and the note that final files transfer after full payment. Even a clearly written, confirmed WhatsApp or email summary is far better than nothing.</p><p>Your capstone begins here: identify one real local business, run a discovery conversation, and write a genuine brief with a quoted price. Whether or not money changes hands this first time, you are now practicing the exact commercial process of a professional studio.</p>","21":"<p>Clients rarely leave because a design was slightly imperfect; they leave because the process felt chaotic, missed deadlines, confusing files, defensive reactions to feedback. A calm, repeatable workflow is what turns one-time customers into repeat clients who recommend you across Monrovia and beyond. This lesson gives you that workflow end to end.</p><h4>The Five-Stage Project Flow</h4><ul><li><strong>1. Confirm:</strong> brief agreed, deposit received, deadline written down.</li><li><strong>2. Create:</strong> design the agreed concepts. Work in organized files, name them clearly, like \"BenduCatering_Flyer_v2.png\", never \"final_FINAL_new2.png\".</li><li><strong>3. Present:</strong> send watermarked or low-resolution previews with a short note explaining your choices, and give a clear feedback deadline (\"Please send all changes by Thursday\").</li><li><strong>4. Revise:</strong> collect feedback in one consolidated list per round, apply it, and track which of the included rounds has been used.</li><li><strong>5. Deliver:</strong> on final payment, send the complete file package.</li></ul><h4>Handling Feedback Gracefully</h4><p>Vague feedback (\"make it pop\") needs translation: ask whether they mean stronger contrast, brighter colors, or a bigger headline. When a client requests something that harms the design, six fonts, a stretched logo, explain the principle briefly, show both versions if needed, then respect their final decision. You are the expert advisor; they own the brand.</p><h4>The Professional Delivery Package</h4><p>What you hand over defines how professional you seem. A complete logo delivery includes: PNG with transparent background (full color, white, and black versions), JPG on white, a print-ready PDF, the exact color codes and font names, and, for full identity projects, the brand guidelines document from Module 5. Deliver in a tidy zipped folder or organized Google Drive link with subfolders like \"Logo/Print\" and \"Logo/Digital\". Keep your own archive of every project; clients will lose their files and return to you, which is another service opportunity.</p><h4>Reliability Is the Brand</h4><p>Reply to messages within a working day, warn early if a deadline is at risk, and deliver when promised. In any market, and especially in tight-knit business communities like Liberia's, reputation travels by word of mouth faster than any advert. Continue your capstone: take your 6.1 brief through all five stages and assemble a full, organized delivery package.</p>","22":"<p>You now have design principles, Canva and Photoshop production skills, a brand identity system, and a professional client workflow. The final step is packaging all of it into a career that grows, locally in Liberia, remotely for international clients, or inside an organization as an in-house designer.</p><h4>Assemble a Portfolio That Sells</h4><p>Choose your 6-10 strongest pieces, anchored by your Module 5 brand identity project presented as a full case study: the brief, the process sketches, the final logo suite, the guidelines, and mockups. For each piece write three short lines, the client or scenario, the goal, and what the design achieved. Host it in the way that fits your market: a free Behance profile for international reach, a Canva-built PDF portfolio for emailing to Liberian companies and NGOs, and a consistently branded Facebook or Instagram page where local clients actually look. Your own social pages are themselves a portfolio, design them beautifully.</p><h4>Choose a Direction</h4><ul><li><strong>Local freelance:</strong> flyers, branding, and campaign graphics for businesses, churches, schools, and NGOs. Advantage: relationships and mobile-money-easy payments.</li><li><strong>Remote freelance:</strong> platforms like Upwork and Fiverr pay in US dollars. Compete by specializing, \"brand identity for food businesses\" wins more than \"I design everything\", and by delivering reliably with excellent written English.</li><li><strong>In-house roles:</strong> telecoms, banks, media houses, and NGOs hire designers for steady salaries. They look for portfolio quality, brand consistency skills, and production knowledge (print specs from Module 4 matter here).</li></ul><h4>Keep Compounding Your Skills</h4><p>Next skills that multiply a designer's income: video and motion graphics for social media (huge demand), UI design for apps and websites (pairs with TIH's Web Development course), and marketing knowledge (TIH's Digital Marketing course) so your designs sell, not just decorate. Follow real design work daily, Behance, Instagram design accounts, and the brands you admire, and recreate strong designs as private practice.</p><h4>Your 30-Day Launch Plan</h4><p>Week 1: finish your capstone case study and portfolio. Week 2: publish your branded pages and portfolio PDF. Week 3: pitch five real organizations with a tailored sample. Week 4: deliver your first project using the full Module 6 workflow. You began this course learning what contrast means; you finish it as a working designer with a process, a portfolio, and a plan. Congratulations, now go create.</p>","23":"<p>The Final Assessment spans the <strong>entire course</strong>: design principles, color and typography, Canva production, Photoshop editing, print and digital specifications, brand identity systems and logo process from Module 5, and the freelance business skills from Module 6.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong> and earn your TIH certificate, with <strong>unlimited retries</strong>, so take a breath and trust your training. You have built real designs, a real brand identity, and a real professional process. Pass this assessment, add the certificate to your portfolio, and start designing for clients. You are ready!</p>"};
